From 7d6deb53cbce1dea52710db7eb882949d89571cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Intel MPSS <mpss@intel.invalid>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 09:57:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 03/16] x86/k1om: add the Intel MIC (l1om/k1om) architecture
 plumbing

From Intel MPSS 3.8.6 (Linux 2.6.38 card kernel). ARCH=l1om/k1om build
arch/x86 for the Knights Ferry / Knights Corner coprocessor cores:
 - ELF machine types EM_L1OM (180) / EM_K1OM (181): ELF_ARCH, kexec
   vmcore check, the ptrace regset view name, OUTPUT_FORMAT
   elf64-{l,k}1om for vmlinux, the decompressor and the vDSO; the
   recordmcount and sorttable host tools treat EM_K1OM like x86-64.
 - Kconfig: X86_MICPCI platform (selects ML1OM/MK1OM from $(ARCH),
   virtio for the out-of-tree card block driver), X86_EARLYMIC for the
   in-order MIC cores, MIC PHYSICAL_START/ALIGN.
 - Makefile: UTS_MACHINE from $(ARCH), CHECKFLAGS -m64, the
   MIC_KERNEL_VERSION_* defsym the MPSS card tools look for.

Adaptations for 6.18:
 - ML1OM/MK1OM sit outside the processor-family choice, which is
   32-bit only since 6.15 (depends on X86_MICPCI && X86_64); the whole
   KNC config silently dropped out otherwise.
 - Kconfig tests use "$(ARCH)" (4.18 no longer imports env symbols).
 - sorttable.c (new host tool since 3.5) gets the EM_K1OM case.
 - Dropped from the MPSS delta: the out-of-tree KDB debugger (never
   built here), the MPSS uconfig fragments, scripts/bin2c, and the
   VECTOR_MEMCPY option (depends on a Kconfig symbol that no longer
   exists; its copy_user_generic_vector.S was never linked), the
   X86_MIC_EMULATION hacks for Intel's pre-silicon emulator, K1OM_TEST
   (KNC power management on a Knights Ferry) and MPSS's own
   PARALLEL_AP_BOOT (superseded by the upstream parallel bringup, which
   works on KNC since 6.5).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Signed-off-by: Anselm Busse <anselm.busse@outlook.com>
